import "core/compile.ct"


module(Catln/Doc)
  # # Catln WebDocs
    This provides common components and annotations used in Webdocs.
    These include the ones
  class(DShow)
    # Represents objects which can be shown in the webdocs.
  class(DocPage)
    # Marks an annotation as one the webdocs offer as a page of its own, rather than one only written
      into a program beside the thing it is about. A reader is offered these by name — in the search
      box, without having to know they exist — so what is marked here is what there is to find, and
      an annotation added below is found by marking it and nothing else.
      What is left unmarked is as much of what this says as what is marked, since a reader offered a
      page they have no use for is a reader reading past it. Three kinds are left out. A display of
      whatever it is written on ([#md], [#print], [#test]) is not a page but part of one, and has
      nothing to show asked for on its own. A display of the doc server's own furniture ([#sidebar],
      [#search]) is already on the reader's screen, so offering it in the box it is drawn in says
      nothing. And a page about one program rather than about the pages there are ([#typeInfer]) has
      to be pointed at that program to mean anything, so it belongs where a page is already in hand
      rather than in a box offering the same thing wherever it is opened from.
      The last of those is about what the page says rather than about which program it is of, so a
      page about a program which still says something of every page there is belongs in the box.
      [#prgmGraph] is the case: pointed at a program it draws that program, and pointed at nothing it
      draws which pages the doc server holds and how they reach one another, which is worth opening
      from anywhere.
      Membership says the annotation is a page, not that it can be written with no argument. What can
      be offered without a reader typing anything is worked out from the declaration: an annotation
      taking no arguments is offered once, and one whose arguments are all sealed classes is offered
      once per combination of their members. An annotation taking a [String] — a type name, a
      function to run — names something only the reader knows, so marking it offers nothing until
      there is somewhere to type that in.

  dshow(s -> DShow)= s
    # [dshow] resolves a value or annotation to its [DShow] display form. The identity arm passes a
      value that is already [DShow] straight through; the arms below turn compiler annotations (like
      [#md] or [#type]) into their visualization types.

  every(String, isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])
  every(/Data/ToString, isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])
    # Every [/Data/ToString] value is displayable, so [ToString] is a subset of [DShow]: the
      webdocs render such a value through its [/Data/toString]. This is what lets a `> value`
      print statement accept any [ToString] type. A [DShow] value that is not [ToString] (a
      visualization like [Show/MD]) is still printable, but only [ToString] values are compared
      against a print/result golden.

  data(Show/MD(text -> String))

  every(Show/MD.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #md(text))= Show/MD(text= text)
  dshow(s= #print(p))= p

  data(Show/Html(contents -> String))
    # [Show/Html] shows markup a program wrote, laid into the page it is shown on. It is what a
      library reaches for to say how one of its own types is displayed: an arm of [dshow] written
      beside the declaration builds the markup, and the webdocs render it and know nothing else about
      it. So a display belongs to the library owning the type rather than to a component of the doc
      server named after that type.
      Laid in and not framed, which is what separates it from the frame a compiled result is shown
      in. That frames because what is in it is a whole document with its own styling, and laying one
      into this page would take this page's styling and give it that document's. A program's own
      display is not a document of its own, so it is meant to take this page's font, its links and
      its colours, which a frame would give it none of.
      An entry of a feed is laid in too, and is the case where that is a judgement rather than an
      obvious one. What an entry says is markup somebody else wrote, which was framed and sandboxed
      while the doc server held a display of its own for it. Written as an arm here it is laid in
      like any other markup an arm builds, so it loses that isolation and keeps only the sanitizing —
      which is what drops the scripts and the handlers, and is the half of the pair that was doing
      the work. What it costs is that an entry's own styling now reads against this page's rather
      than beside it, and that is the price of a feed saying what its entries look like instead of
      the doc server saying it.
      Markup where [Show/MD] is prose: writing which reads as a document is markdown, and writing
      which is a shape to lay out is this, because markdown cannot say how a heading or a table sits.
      Nothing written here is escaped, and the webdocs sanitize what they are given before showing
      it. An arm joining a string into markup is an arm whose string is read as markup, which is that
      arm's to get right; the sanitizing keeps getting it wrong from being a page that runs what a
      string said.

  every(Show/Html.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  annot(#overlay(url -> String))

  data(Show/Overlay(url -> String))
    # [Show/Overlay] shows a page written over another one: the writing of the page carrying it
      above, and the page at [url] below. It carries the address alone, because what is at the
      other end is not the doc server's to compile, to hold, or to answer for — the browser
      fetches it itself, from whoever serves it. So this is the one display that says where to
      look rather than what was found there, and the one the webdocs read as a shape to lay a page
      out in rather than as a value to show in the flow of it.
      A site may refuse to be shown inside another page, and refuses in a header the page framing
      it is not allowed to read. So the address is shown as a link wherever it appears, and the
      annotation itself displays as that link rather than as a frame of its own: the frame belongs
      to the page laying itself out around it, so a page carrying an overlay is one frame and one
      link rather than two frames, and a reader is never left with a blank frame and no way
      through to what it was for.

  every(Show/Overlay.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #overlay(url))= Show/Overlay(url= url)

  class(ListProgramMode, [Desugar, Typecheck, TreeBuild])
    # Which compiler stage a [#listProgram] lists: the program before typechecking, after
      typechecking, or after tree building.

  annot(#listProgram(mode -> ListProgramMode), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DocPage])
    # Offered as a page per [ListProgramMode], since the mode is the whole of what it takes and the
      class names every one there is.
      The class is named absolutely, as every [isa] in this file is. A relative name here is not
      resolved: the extends list is keyed into the class graph as it is written, so @DocPage@ becomes
      a second class of that name beside this one rather than a reference to it.

  data(Show/ListProgram(prgm -> String, mode -> ListProgramMode))
    # [Show/ListProgram] lists every declaration and definition of a program. It carries the
      program it lists in [prgm], so the webdocs render the page from the compiled value itself
      rather than asking the compiler for it over a separate route. The [mode] it was listed for
      travels with it, so the page's stage selector reads the annotation rather than holding its
      own state.

  every(Show/ListProgram.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #listProgram(mode)) =
    listProgram(annot) -> String
      # [listProgram] renders the program being evaluated as JSON, at the compiler stage its
        [mode] names. The program is the one the annotation was evaluated against, so a
        [#listProgram] in a page lists that page and everything it reaches, at every stage alike.
      #runtime("listProgram")

    Show/ListProgram(prgm= listProgram(annot= s), mode= mode)

  annot(#type(name -> String))

  data(Show/TypePage(prgm -> String, name -> String))
    # [Show/TypePage] lists the declarations and definitions of a single type or class. Like
      [Show/ListProgram] it carries the listing it shows in [prgm], so the webdocs render the page
      from the compiled value rather than asking the compiler for it over a separate route. The
      [name] it was filtered by travels with it so the page can title itself.

  every(Show/TypePage.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #type(name)) =
    filterProgram(annot) -> String
      # [filterProgram] renders the declarations and definitions of [name] as JSON. It is
        [listProgram] narrowed to one name, over the same program the annotation was evaluated
        against.
      #runtime("filterProgram")

    Show/TypePage(prgm= filterProgram(annot= s), name= name)

  annot(#typeInfer)

  data(Show/TypeInfer(constrain -> String))
    # [Show/TypeInfer] shows how the program's types were inferred. It carries that program in
      [constrain], with a type variable on every meta, so the webdocs render the page from the
      compiled value rather than asking the compiler for it over a separate route.

  every(Show/TypeInfer.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #typeInfer) =
    typeInfer(annot) -> String
      # [typeInfer] renders the type inference of the program being evaluated as JSON, paired with
        the notes its typecheck produced. Like [listProgram] it reports on whatever program the
        annotation was evaluated against. The trace of how each point was solved is not included;
        a point is asked for on its own through [#typeInferPnt].
      #runtime("typeInfer")

    Show/TypeInfer(constrain= typeInfer(annot= s))

  annot(#typeInferPnt(pnt -> Integer))

  data(Show/TypeInferPnt(trace -> String, pnt -> Integer))
    # [Show/TypeInferPnt] shows how one point of the type inference was solved: the constraints
      mentioning it, and what each epoch updated it to. It is the drill-down of a [Show/TypeInfer],
      which is a program's worth of points, and so is shown embedded within one. The [pnt] it was
      filtered by travels with it so the page can title itself.

  every(Show/TypeInferPnt.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #typeInferPnt(pnt)) =
    typeInferPnt(annot) -> String
      # [typeInferPnt] renders the part of the same trace that touches [pnt] as JSON. Only that
        part: the whole trace records every update of every point in every epoch, and would dwarf
        the program it describes.
      #runtime("typeInferPnt")

    Show/TypeInferPnt(trace= typeInferPnt(annot= s), pnt= pnt)

  annot(#prgmGraph, isa= [/Catln/Doc/DocPage])
    # The pages of the program it is written in, drawn as the graph of which of them import which.
      It takes nothing, as [/Data/Calendar/#calendar] takes nothing and for its reason: what to draw
      is what is there. The program is the page it is written in and everything that page imports,
      and written where nothing is scoped it is every page the doc server holds.
      Marked as a page of its own, which [#typeInfer] above is not. The paragraph on [DocPage] leaves
      a page about one program out of the box because it has to be pointed at a program to mean
      anything, and that holds of this one written in a page. It does not hold of this one written in
      none: a graph of every page there is and how they reach one another is about which pages there
      are, which is the question [#sidebar] and [#search] answer in the shapes they answer it in, and
      is worth opening from wherever a reader is. So it is offered by name, and a reader who wants
      the graph of one program opens it from that program.

  data(Show/PrgmGraph(graph -> String))
    # [Show/PrgmGraph] shows a program as its pages and the imports between them: a node per page,
      carrying the path it is opened by, the pages it imports, whether it belongs to the standard
      library, and whether it is the page the graph was drawn for. It carries all of that in [graph],
      so the webdocs draw the graph from the compiled value rather than asking the compiler for it
      over a separate route.
      The standard library is in it, marked, rather than left out. A program's own pages and the
      library it rests on are two questions asked of one graph, and a graph answering only one of
      them would have to be drawn again to answer the other. Which of them a reader is asking is
      decided where the graph is drawn, as [Show/Calendar] decides there which days it draws and for
      the same reason: that is a reader turning over what they have already been handed.

  every(Show/PrgmGraph.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #prgmGraph) =
    prgmGraph(annot) -> String
      # [prgmGraph] renders the pages the evaluation covers as JSON: each the path it is opened by,
        the paths of the pages it imports, and whether it is the standard library's and whether it is
        the one the evaluation is of. Every page a page imports is itself one of the pages, since a
        page is covered because something reached it and what it reaches is reached too.
      #runtime("prgmGraph")

    Show/PrgmGraph(graph= prgmGraph(annot= s))

  annot(#sidebar)

  data(Show/Sidebar(pages -> String))
    # [Show/Sidebar] shows the pages a doc server holds, as the tree of directories they sit in. It
      carries that tree in [pages], so the webdocs render their navigation from the compiled value
      rather than asking the compiler for it over a separate route. Unlike the pages above it is not
      about the program it is evaluated against but about which pages there are to evaluate against
      at all, so, like [Show/Search] below, it is meant to be evaluated unscoped.

  every(Show/Sidebar.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #sidebar) =
    sidebar(annot) -> String
      # [sidebar] renders the pages the evaluation covers as a JSON tree: a node per directory and
        per file, each carrying the path to link it by. Evaluated unscoped that is every page the
        doc server loaded, which is what a navigation of the whole doc server wants.
      #runtime("sidebar")

    Show/Sidebar(pages= sidebar(annot= s))

  annot(#search)

  data(Show/Search(pages -> String))
    # [Show/Search] shows the pages a reader can look for, as the flat list of the paths they are
      opened by. It carries that list in [pages], so the webdocs search from the compiled value
      rather than from whichever navigation happens to be on screen. Like [Show/Sidebar] it is about
      which pages there are rather than about the program it was evaluated against, so a search of
      the whole doc server is evaluated unscoped; written in a page it searches what that page
      reaches.

  every(Show/Search.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  searchWebdocs(query -> String) -> DShow =
    # [searchWebdocs] is the search itself: the pages whose path [query] matches, as the box that
      opens the one that is picked. It is a function rather than an annotation because the webdocs
      call it as the reader types, over @/api/call@, passing what they have typed — an annotation is
      written into a program and so can only carry what was written there.
      An empty [query] matches every page, which is what a box that has not been typed into shows.
    searchPagesFor(query -> String) -> String
      # [searchPagesFor] renders the pages the evaluation covers whose path holds [query] as a JSON
        list of those paths. They are the pages [sidebar] renders as a tree; a search box wants the
        flat list it offers rather than a navigation to walk.
      #runtime("searchPagesFor")

    Show/Search(pages= searchPagesFor(query= query))

  dshow(s= #search)= searchWebdocs(query= "")

  data(Show/DocPages(pages -> String))
    # [Show/DocPages] shows the annotations that are pages in their own right — the ones marked
      [DocPage] — as the list a reader picks one from. It carries that list in [pages], each entry
      the source to run and the label to offer it under.
      Like [Show/Search] it is about what there is to ask for rather than about the program it was
      evaluated against. Unlike it, what there is comes from the program rather than from which files
      were read: an annotation is a page by being marked as one where it is declared, so this answers
      about whichever program it is evaluated against and is asked of the whole doc server the same
      way a search is.

  every(Show/DocPages.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  docPages(query -> String) -> DShow =
    # [docPages] is the pages that are annotations rather than files: those whose source or label
      [query] matches, as the list that opens the one that is picked. A function rather than an
      annotation for the reason [searchWebdocs] is one — the webdocs call it as the reader types,
      passing what they have typed.
      An empty [query] matches every one, which is what a box that has not been typed into offers.
      That is a handful of entries rather than every page there is, which is why a box asks this
      before anything is typed and asks [searchWebdocs] only once something has been.
    docPagesFor(query -> String) -> String
      # [docPagesFor] renders the annotations marked [DocPage] whose source or label holds [query] as
        a JSON list of what to run and what to call it. An annotation taking no arguments is one
        entry; one taking only arguments whose values a sealed class names is an entry per
        combination of them, which is how the three stages of a [#listProgram] are three pages; one
        taking a value only the reader knows is none, since there is no list to offer.
      #runtime("docPagesFor")

    Show/DocPages(pages= docPagesFor(query= query))

  data(Show/Test(run -> String))
    # [Show/Test] shows how the tests of one definition ran: what each of them was, whether it
      passed, and how many generated cases it got through. It carries that run in [run], so the
      webdocs show a test's result beside the test itself rather than asking the compiler for it
      over a separate route.
      Unlike the displays above it has no annotation of its own. It is what [#test] shows: a [#test]
      already marks a definition as a test, so the definitions it is written on are exactly the ones
      with a run to show.

  every(Show/Test.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #test) =
    testRun(annot) -> String
      # [testRun] renders the tests of the definition the annotation is written on as JSON. Of that
        definition, not of the page: a [#test] marks one definition as a test, so it reports on that
        one.
      #runtime("testRun")

    Show/Test(run= testRun(annot= s))

  annot(#debugger(fun -> String))

  data(Show/Debugger(run -> String, fun -> String))
    # [Show/Debugger] shows a run of one of the program's functions: what it evaluated, what it
      printed, and what it exited with. It carries that run in [run], so the webdocs render the page
      from the compiled value rather than asking the compiler for it over a separate route. The
      [fun] it ran travels with it so the page can title itself.
      It shows a run of a single expression ([#debugExpr]) just as well, since what distinguishes
      the two is what the run ended with rather than how it is read, and [run] carries that. So
      [fun] is what was run, which for an expression is the expression itself.

  every(Show/Debugger.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  dshow(s= #debugger(fun)) =
    debugger(annot) -> String
      # [debugger] runs [fun] in the program being evaluated and renders the run as JSON. The
        program's output is captured rather than printed, so showing a run inside a doc server does
        not write the run's output to the server's own stdout.
      #runtime("debugger")

    Show/Debugger(run= debugger(annot= s), fun= fun)

  annot(#debugExpr(expr -> String))

  dshow(s= #debugExpr(expr)) =
    debugExpr(annot) -> String
      # [debugExpr] compiles [expr] as though it were written in the page being evaluated, runs it,
        and renders the run as JSON. An expression rather than a function name, which is what
        [#debugger] takes: what a reader wants to debug is often something that no definition names
        — the computation a [#print] displays the result of, or the one generated input a test case
        ran on. Neither exists in the program as a name to run.
        Its own annotation rather than a second form of [#debugger] because the two run different
        things in different ways, and an annotation whose meaning turns on which argument was given
        reads as one thing that sometimes does another.
      #runtime("debugExpr")

    Show/Debugger(run= debugExpr(annot= s), fun= expr)
